{
  "version": 1,
  "summary": "Each run owns its instance for its process life, and stateless instances share one ledger of immutable, self-checking passes through VERILEX_LEDGER.",
  "files": [
    {
      "path": "internal/runner/runner.go",
      "summary": "Gives each run a unique id and a lock it holds while it lives, and refuses a losing --continue before anything starts.",
      "annotations": [
        {
          "newRange": [
            63,
            88
          ],
          "summary": "reserve creates the run directory with an exclusive Mkdir under a 6-byte random id, and Refused marks a run that must leave no record.",
          "rationale": "Run ids label the instance a run launches, so two concurrent runs must never share one. The old 3-byte suffix and MkdirAll let two runs in one second collide silently. Six bytes also keep runs from different state homes apart in a shared ledger. Proven by TestConcurrentRunsEachDriveOnlyTheirOwnInstance and verify-verilex parallel.md (session LmBsD0). A collision retry is not exercised by any test, because it needs a forced id.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-shared-ledger"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            99,
            103
          ],
          "summary": "Execute removes the new run's directory and returns Refused when handOver refuses the kept instance.",
          "rationale": "Behavior change: a losing --continue used to record an inconclusive run. Because nothing was launched and no word ran, the run now leaves nothing in verilex runs. Proven by TestKeptInstanceGoesToExactlyOneContinuingRun and the parallel.md one-taker step with 6 concurrent --continue runs.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-shared-ledger"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            135,
            140
          ],
          "summary": "handOver takes the kept run through Claim, so a run that is still going or already continued is refused before any word runs.",
          "rationale": "Claim reads the record under an exclusive lock, so exactly one concurrent --continue wins. The loser prints either 'was continued by' or 'in use by another verilex command', depending on timing; both are refusals.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-shared-ledger"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            213,
            214
          ],
          "summary": "entry records the claim version the word proved and the run that launched the instance it drove.",
          "rationale": "The ledger gate refuses a pass whose Owner differs from its Run, so only a run that launched its own instance can record a pass. A continued run drives another run's instance, so its results stay in history and never become passes.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-shared-ledger"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "path": "internal/runner/plan.go",
      "summary": "Owns hold, the single rule for when a command may read, tear down or take over a recorded run.",
      "annotations": [
        {
          "newRange": [
            118,
            165
          ],
          "summary": "hold refuses a run that is still pending and busy at once, and waits up to 500 ms for any other short holder.",
          "rationale": "A busy lock on a pending record means the run's own process lives, so waiting would not help. Readers take a shared lock, so concurrent plans never refuse each other; the first version used exclusive probes and 12 concurrent plans refused each other. A killed process releases its flock, so cleanup still reaches a dead run. Proven by the parallel_test.go tests and parallel.md dead-run and plan steps.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-shared-ledger"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "path": "internal/runner/execute.go",
      "summary": "Replaces the blocking lock with non-blocking shared or exclusive flocks and a liveness probe.",
      "annotations": [
        {
          "newRange": [
            101,
            111
          ],
          "summary": "locked reports a live run when a shared non-blocking flock on run.lock fails with EWOULDBLOCK.",
          "rationale": "The probe opens without O_CREATE, so a missing lock file reads as not running and never creates state. A shared probe can also conflict with a brief exclusive holder such as cleanup, so verilex runs may show running for that instant; it never shows died for a live run.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-shared-ledger"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "path": "internal/cli/cli.go",
      "summary": "Wires the refusal, the died status and the cleanup claim into the commands.",
      "annotations": [
        {
          "newRange": [
            72,
            73
          ],
          "summary": "verilex runs shows died for a run with no verdict whose process no longer holds its lock.",
          "rationale": "Behavior change: a killed run used to show running forever. Proven by TestCleanupTearsDownTheInstanceOfARunThatDied and parallel.md dead-run step (session LmBsD0).",
          "author": "fm/verilex-shared-ledger"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [
            342,
            347
          ],
          "summary": "cleanup takes the run through Claim and holds it until teardown ends.",
          "rationale": "Behavior change: cleanup now refuses a run that is still going, because tearing down a live run's instance would break that run. Cleanup of one run never touches another run's store, which parallel.md cleanup-isolated proves.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-shared-ledger"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
